WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:05.076
IIIF Kasane: comparing provincial maps (Tenpō kuniezu, Ōmi) — Opening it from Use cases

2
00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:13.679
IIIF Kasane is a viewer that layers IIIF images, 3D models and annotations in a single view.

3
00:00:13.682 --> 00:00:18.482
The Use cases page lists examples with real collections.

4
00:00:18.494 --> 00:00:22.944
In this video, we use the Tenpō provincial map of Ōmi.

5
00:00:22.948 --> 00:00:29.498
Kuniezu are large maps of each province, made at the order of the Tokugawa shogunate.

6
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.450
The first is the official map of Ōmi, held by the National Archives of Japan.

7
00:00:35.454 --> 00:00:43.579
The second is a copy of the same size, made a year earlier. It is held by Stanford University Libraries.

8
00:00:43.581 --> 00:00:48.531
Click "Open in Kasane" to open a page for comparing the two.

9
00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:54.676
IIIF Kasane: comparing provincial maps (Tenpō kuniezu, Ōmi) — Comparing with a swipe

10
00:00:54.692 --> 00:01:00.892
This is the "Swipe" view. A is shown left of the divider, and B to the right.

11
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:06.849
A is the official map from the National Archives, and B the copy from Stanford.

12
00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:12.079
Both maps are drawn the same way, with east at the top and north on the left.

13
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:18.759
Drag the divider to the left, and the copy spreads out. Lake Biwa is left pale.

14
00:01:18.764 --> 00:01:26.939
Drag it to the right for the official map. The lake is painted deep blue, and the neighbouring provinces are coloured too.

15
00:01:29.394 --> 00:01:33.669
Zoom in on the south-west shore of the lake with the mouse wheel.

16
00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:43.379
The ovals that stand for villages are filled in red on the official map. Around here on the copy, they are left blank.

17
00:01:43.386 --> 00:01:48.436
The two maps are not aligned, so they drift apart a little when zoomed in.

18
00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:52.266
The divider can also run across, with "Up–down".

19
00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:59.043
IIIF Kasane: comparing provincial maps (Tenpō kuniezu, Ōmi) — Choosing images and modes

20
00:01:59.045 --> 00:02:03.170
"Side by side" puts the two maps next to each other.

21
00:02:03.176 --> 00:02:06.276
Both sides zoom and move together.

22
00:02:08.036 --> 00:02:14.936
In "Spyglass", B is shown only inside a round window. Move it to peek at the copy.

23
00:02:14.941 --> 00:02:20.166
The B menu lists three images from Stanford University Libraries.

24
00:02:20.171 --> 00:02:25.446
The second shows the outside of the folded map, with its title written on it.

25
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:33.008
Click "Sources" to list what is open: one Collection, with four images.

26
00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:38.261
Type another URL below and click "Add" to compare more sources.

27
00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:44.959
IIIF Kasane: comparing provincial maps (Tenpō kuniezu, Ōmi) — Where the data comes from

28
00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:49.638
This comparison starts from a single IIIF Collection.

29
00:02:49.641 --> 00:02:57.341
It holds the two Manifests published by the National Archives of Japan and by Stanford University Libraries.

30
00:02:57.347 --> 00:03:01.847
Click "Open in the viewer" to open A in the ordinary viewer.

31
00:03:03.063 --> 00:03:10.888
The panel on the right shows the catalogue record of the National Archives. The date is 1838.

32
00:03:10.892 --> 00:03:15.167
Stanford's record dates the copy to 1837.

33
00:03:15.169 --> 00:03:19.294
For the terms of use, please see each institution's site.
